C = [
("h1", "16. Objections and Replies"),
("p", "This chapter takes the strongest objections to the position and answers each from Scripture. The objections are stated as their holders state them. One modern writer is named whose published words the author has read in full on a free text, Thomas L. Constable, in his notes on Galatians, Romans, Colossians and 2 Corinthians. He is quoted only from those texts. He fortifies the thesis at some points and holds the opposite view at others, and the chapter says which is which. The other objections are held by traditions whose individual writers were not read for this volume. They are stated in the volume’s words, and no sentence is placed in any person’s mouth."),
("sub", "16.1 Objection: the Cross is only a demonstration of God’s love"),
("p", "This reading holds that the death of Christ changes people by moving them, and that nothing was done by it that needed to be done. It rests on passages like Romans 5:8, “God commendeth his love toward us, in that, while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us.” It is held by a tradition that stresses the example of Christ, and it takes the other passages as pictures of the same effect."),
("p", "The volume replies that Romans 5:8 is real and says what it says: the death commends God’s love. But the next verses say more. “Much more then, being now justified by his blood, we shall be saved from wrath through him. For if, when we were enemies, we were reconciled to God by the death of his Son” (Romans 5:9, 10). The verbs are justified, saved, reconciled, and the means is the blood and the death. Galatians 3:13 says Christ was “made a curse for us,” which is an act done, and Colossians 2:14 says the handwriting was blotted out and nailed “to his cross.” An example moves a person. These verses say that something was removed. The volume holds that the demonstration is part of what Paul says and not the whole."),
("sub", "16.2 Objection: the Cross is a victory over the powers and nothing else"),
("p", "This reading takes Colossians 2:15 as the heart of the matter and reads the other passages as parts of the same triumph. It holds that the powers held men by a claim and that the Cross broke it. Thomas Constable reads the verse in a way that supports the first half: the disarming of the rulers “probably refers to Christ’s defeat of Satan and his evil demonic assistants by His death and resurrection.”"),
("p", "The volume agrees that Colossians 2:15 says the powers were spoiled and triumphed over. It does not identify the powers as Satan and his demons, because the verse names them only as “principalities and powers,” and the volume does not draw a map of the unseen world from two words. It also asks what the verse is a verse of. It stands in the same sentence as the forgiveness of all trespasses and the blotting out of a handwriting. It is one of three figures. The victory reading takes one figure as the whole. Galatians 3:13 speaks of no powers, and Romans 3:25 speaks of the righteousness of God. The reading explains one passage well and the others by compression."),
("sub", "16.3 Objection: propitiation should be expiation"),
("p", "This objection holds that the word in Romans 3:25 means the removal of guilt and not the turning aside of wrath, and that the King James Version has taken the wrong sense. Constable states the choice plainly. He notes that the Greek word could refer to “the place” where God satisfied his wrath, or to the sacrifice itself, and he prefers the second: “Jesus Christ Himself as the sacrifice that satisfied God’s wrath and removed our sins.” He adds, “Jesus Christ was the sacrifice, but the place where God made atonement was the Cross.”"),
("p", "The volume uses the King James word and does not argue the Greek. It notes that Constable himself sees both senses in the verse, and that his reading joins them: the sacrifice satisfied wrath and removed sins. The volume’s claim is narrower. The verse says God set Christ forth to be a propitiation to declare his righteousness. It does not define the word, and the surrounding sentences name the problem as the righteousness of God. Whether the English word is turned toward wrath or toward sin, the sentence gives the purpose and leaves the mechanism."),
("sub", "16.4 Objection: Paul does not teach that Christ bore the penalty in our place"),
("p", "This is the objection of those who hold that the language of penalty is read into Paul from later systems. It points out that no sentence of Paul says that Christ was punished in our place, and that the word “for” can mean on behalf of as well as instead of."),
("p", "The volume agrees that no sentence in the passages studied contains the phrase //punished in our place//. It agrees that the word “for” does not settle the sense by itself. It replies that Paul does say that Christ was “made a curse for us” (Galatians 3:13), that God “made him to be sin for us” (2 Corinthians 5:21), and that God “condemned sin in the flesh” (Romans 8:3). These statements stand. The volume does not turn them into a theory of penalty, and it does not remove them. Constable reads Galatians 3:13 as Christ taking “the wrath of God that was directed toward us upon Himself.” The volume reports the words in the verses and declines to say more than they say, either for or against the objection."),
("sub", "16.5 Objection: the extent of the Cross is limited, or is universal"),
("p", "Two opposite objections meet here. One holds that “for all” in 2 Corinthians 5:14 means all kinds of persons, and that the Cross was for the elect only. The other holds that “for all” means every person, and that the Cross made salvation possible for each. Constable’s notes contain both strands. On Galatians 3:13 he writes that Christ “paid the penalty for our sins and made justification possible for every person.” On 2 Corinthians 5:14 he writes that “all believers died in the Person of their representative, Jesus Christ.”"),
("p", "The volume replies that neither doctrine is stated in the clauses. “One died for all” is said, and “then were all dead” follows. The purpose clause turns at once to “they which live.” “For us” in Romans 5:8 and Galatians 3:13, and “for the ungodly” in Romans 5:6, are used of readers and of a type. “For all” in 1 Timothy 2:6 is joined to “one God, and one mediator between God and men.” The volume does not choose between the two objections, and it observes that a single commentator, reading carefully, can find both senses in different verses. That is some evidence that the verses do not decide the matter in their own words."),
("sub", "16.6 Objection: Galatians 3:13 is only about Israel’s curse"),
("p", "This reading takes “us” in Galatians 3:13 to be Jewish believers and “the curse of the law” to be the curse that had come upon Israel under the covenant. It holds that Gentiles are named separately in verse 14 and receive the blessing without having been under the curse. No writer is named here, because none was read for this volume, and the objection is stated in the volume’s words."),
("p", "The volume agrees with a great deal of this. “The curse of the law” is the curse the law pronounces on those who are under it, and Constable describes it as “the curse pronounced by the Law on the law-breaker.” The Gentiles of verse 14 are named as those on whom the blessing comes. But the volume does not say that “us” in verse 13 can be fixed more narrowly than Paul fixes it, and it does not say that the Galatians were under the law’s curse. It reports the words and stops. The objection is not refuted; it is not needed to read the passage."),
("sub", "16.7 Objection: the baptism of Romans 6 is water baptism"),
("p", "This reading takes “baptized into Jesus Christ” and “buried with him by baptism into death” as the rite of water, and the death with Christ as enacted in it. The volume replies that the passage speaks of a baptism “into Jesus Christ” and “into his death,” which the study on the one baptism reads as the baptism by the Spirit, and that Constable on the parallel in Colossians 2:12 writes that “Baptism here (v. 12) refers to Spirit baptism, which is symbolized by water baptism.” The volume follows him in the first half. The rite is not mentioned in the passage."),
("ctbl", "The objections at a glance.", [
    ("Objection", "Who holds it, as read", "The reply in one line", "Where answered"),
    ("The Cross is only a demonstration of love", "A tradition; no writer read, none named", "Romans 5:9, 10 and the other passages say more than a demonstration", "16.1; chapters 4, 5, 8"),
    ("The Cross is a victory over the powers and nothing else", "Thomas Constable reads Colossians 2:15 as the defeat of Satan", "The verse is one of three figures; the powers are not named", "16.2; chapter 8"),
    ("Propitiation should be expiation", "Thomas Constable states both senses and prefers the sacrifice", "The verse gives the purpose and leaves the mechanism", "16.3; chapter 3"),
    ("Paul does not teach penal substitution", "A tradition; no writer read, none named", "The verses on curse, sin and condemnation stand; the volume builds no theory", "16.4; chapters 5, 6, 10"),
    ("The extent is limited, or is universal", "Thomas Constable reads “every person” at Galatians 3:13 and “all believers” at 2 Corinthians 5:14", "The clauses state neither doctrine", "16.5; chapters 6, 15"),
    ("Galatians 3:13 is only about Israel’s curse", "A tradition; no writer read, none named", "“Us” is Paul’s word; the volume reads no further", "16.6; chapter 5"),
    ("The baptism of Romans 6 is water baptism", "Thomas Constable reads Colossians 2:12 as Spirit baptism, symbolized by water", "The rite is not named in the passage", "16.7; chapter 7"),
  ], [0.26, 0.24, 0.32, 0.18]),
]
